Chapter: BoundedNoneAccepted
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Cost Model, the bottom rung, the arm that must COMPILE.
 `bounded-none-exceeded` in `codex/test/errors` proves the refusal; a refusal
 arm on its own cannot tell a working check from one that refuses everything,
 so this is the control.

 `adds` recurses, does arithmetic, compares, and calls `min`, which section
 3.1 measured at zero bytes. It allocates nothing and declares so. The two
 chapters differ in what the callee does with its accumulator: one pushes
 onto a list, this one adds an integer.

Section: A Callee That Allocates Nothing

  sum-to : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-to (n) (i) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else i + sum-to n (i + 1)

Section: The Declaration That Holds

  bounded none adds : Integer -> Integer
  adds (n) = sum-to n 0 + min n 4

Section: A Rung Widened By Measurement, Not By Rule

 `list-set-at` was on the refusing side until 2026-08-19 for one reason: it
 had never been measured. `codex/test/cost/builtin-alloc` measured it at 0
 bytes on a list of 64 and of 256, and `emit-list-set-at` turns out to have no
 allocation path in it at all -- a bounds check, an address, a store, and a
 return of the same pointer. Its `bs-alloc` row says `none` and this
 declaration holds because of that row.

 THIS ARM IS THE ONE THAT FAILS IF THE REGISTRY IS NOT CONSULTED. Every other
 accepted name here is also in `is-rt-safe-builtin`, so the whole chapter
 would still compile with the lookup removed; `list-set-at` is in the registry
 and not in that set, which is what makes it the discriminator.

  set-one : List Integer -> Integer
  set-one (xs) = list-length (list-set-at xs 0 9)

  bounded none seated : List Integer -> Integer
  seated (xs) = set-one xs

Section: Boolean `&` Allocates Nothing, And Is Now Recorded As Such

 `&` is one token doing two jobs, and the type recorded for the expression is
 the only thing that separates them afterwards. Until 2026-08-19 `infer-and`
 recorded on its TEXT arm and not its boolean one, so a boolean `&` looked
 exactly like an expression nothing knew the type of, and every reader of that
 table treats the unknown as the worst case. `bounded none` refused any body
 that joined two conditions, and `punctual` refused the same shape through
 `check-rt-no-alloc`; both were charging a boolean AND for a heap allocation
 it never makes.

 All three shapes are here because the defect was found by all three failing
 together, which is what ruled out the two theories that came first: it was
 not unresolved type variables and not parameter resolution, because
 `True & False` is as literal as an expression gets and it was refused too.

  bounded none joins-literals : Boolean
  joins-literals = True & False

  bounded none joins-params : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  joins-params (a) (b) = a & b

  bounded none joins-comparisons : Integer -> Boolean
  joins-comparisons (n) = n > 1 & n < 9

Section: The Arithmetic Names The Safe Set Never Held

 `is-rt-safe-builtin` carries the twelve integer and bit names, so a body using
 them was accepted through `punctual`'s set and not through the registry at
 all. `negate`, `real-from-int` and `real-to-int` are in neither, and they were
 refused CDX6101 until `codex/test/cost/builtin-alloc` measured them at 0 bytes
 on 2026-08-21 -- measured against depot `Sut` 96CB73CB, where the whole
 section refuses and the same source compiles once the rows say `none`. These
 three are the discriminators for that run the way `list-set-at` is for the
 section above.

  bounded none flipped : Integer -> Integer
  flipped (n) = negate n

  bounded none rounded : Integer -> Integer
  rounded (n) = real-to-int (real-from-int n)

Section: The Real Conversion Family, Eight Declarations For Sixteen Rows

 The same shape one family further on. None of these sixteen names is in
 `is-rt-safe-builtin`, so every one of them widens `bounded none` and every
 one of them was CDX6101 until `codex/test/cost/builtin-alloc` read its
 ladder at zero on 2026-08-21. The eight declarations pair off exactly as the
 eight arms there do, so each row has an arm that fails without it.

 The kill was taken before the rows moved, against depot `Sut` 9F2789C2:
 all eight refused at exit 4, nothing else in the chapter did, and the same
 source compiles once the sixteen rows read `none`.

 A Real cannot be handed back to a caller comparing integers, so each body
 ends in a conversion back. `real-from-int` and `real-to-int` are already
 `none`, which is why they can carry the ends without deciding the middle.

  bounded none narrowed : Integer -> Integer
  narrowed (n) = real-approx-to-int (real-approx-from-int n)

  bounded none widened : Integer -> Integer
  widened (n) = real-to-int (from-real-approx (to-real-approx (real-from-int n)))

  bounded none trapped : Integer -> Integer
  trapped (n) = real-to-int (from-real-trapping (to-real-trapping (real-from-int n)))

  bounded none saturated : Integer -> Integer
  saturated (n) = real-to-int (from-real-saturating (to-real-saturating (real-from-int n)))

  bounded none approx-trapped : Integer -> Integer
  approx-trapped (n) = real-approx-to-int (from-real-approx-trapping (to-real-approx-trapping (real-approx-from-int n)))

  bounded none approx-saturated : Integer -> Integer
  approx-saturated (n) = real-approx-to-int (from-real-approx-saturating (to-real-approx-saturating (real-approx-from-int n)))

  bounded none punned : Integer -> Integer
  punned (n) = real-to-int (bits-to-real (real-to-bits (real-from-int n)))

  bounded none approx-punned : Integer -> Integer
  approx-punned (n) = real-approx-to-int (bits-to-real-approx (real-approx-to-bits (real-approx-from-int n)))

Section: The SIMD Reads, Which Take Their Vectors As Parameters

 Nine of the seventeen SIMD rows are `none` and every one of them is a READ.
 The other eight allocate a 16-byte box for the vector they produce and are
 `fixed`, so a `bounded none` body cannot construct a vector at all: there is
 no cheap way to make one. Each declaration here therefore takes its vector as
 a parameter, which is what the callers this rung exists for do anyway.

  bounded none lane : Vector 2 Real -> Integer
  lane (v) = real-to-int (vec-extract v 0)

  bounded none lane4 : Vector 4 (Real approximate) -> Integer
  lane4 (q) = real-to-int (from-real-approx (vec4-extract q 0))

  bounded none summed : Vector 2 Real -> Integer
  summed (v) = real-to-int (vec-reduce-add v)

  bounded none stored : Integer, Vector 2 Real -> Integer
  stored (addr) (v) = vec-store-at addr v

  bounded none widthed : Real -> Integer
  widthed (x) = suggested-vector-width x

 THE FOUR MASK PREDICATES GET NO ARM HERE AND CANNOT, which is a fact about
 the type surface rather than about the cost model. `mask-any`, `mask-all`,
 `mask-none` and `mask-count` all take a `VectorMask 2`, and that type has no
 surface syntax: `resolve-applied-type` knows `List`, `LinkedList`, `Real` and
 `Vector`, and anything else becomes an ordinary constructed type. A signature
 naming it is CDX3008 undefined type name, and the mismatch that follows reads
 `VectorMask 2 vs Con:VectorMask[Con:2]`, printing both sides with the same
 name. A mask can therefore only be a local bound from a comparison and cannot
 cross a declaration boundary, so those four rows are measured without a rung.

Section: The Atomics, Where `bs-varies` And `bs-alloc` Come Apart

 All six of these read True for `bs-varies`, because their answer depends on
 what another core did. That is the registry's other column and it decides
 nothing here: measured at zero on 2026-08-21, they retain nothing, so a
 `bounded none` body may call them. A builtin whose RESULT varies is not
 thereby a builtin whose ALLOCATION varies, and these six are the arm that
 says so.

  bounded none peeked : Integer -> Integer
  peeked (addr) = atomic-load addr

  bounded none poked : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  poked (addr) (v) = atomic-store addr v

  bounded none bumped : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bumped (addr) (d) = atomic-add addr d

  bounded none swapped : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  swapped (addr) (v) = atomic-exchange addr v

  bounded none swapped-if : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  swapped-if (addr) (e) (v) = atomic-cas addr e v

  bounded none fenced : Integer -> Integer
  fenced (n) = memory-fence n

Section: The CPU Reads, Including Three That Are Not Calls

 `cpu-read-cr0`, `cpu-read-cr3` and `flush-tlb` are typed plain `Integer` with
 no `FunTy`, so `machined` below has no call in it at all and is the first
 body in this chapter whose builtins are bare names.

 `machined` IS A CONTROL AND IT IS THE ONE ARM HERE THAT DOES NOT MOVE. The
 kill against depot `Sut` D42F8373 refused the other four with CDX6101 and
 accepted this one while all three of its rows still read `unknown`, which is
 the measurement: `cost-head-allocates` is reached from a call HEAD, and a
 nullary builtin is not a call, so the registry is never consulted for these
 three. Their rows are `none` because they were measured at zero, not because
 anything in the checker asked. Written down because the arm was added on the
 opposite assumption and the refusal that did not come is what corrected it.

  bounded none peeked-16 : Integer -> Integer
  peeked-16 (addr) = peek-16 addr 0

  bounded none peeked-qword : Integer -> Integer
  peeked-qword (addr) = peek-qword addr 0

  bounded none poked-16 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  poked-16 (addr) (v) = poke-16 addr 0 v

  bounded none identified : Integer -> Integer
  identified (leaf) = cpu-cpuid-eax leaf + cpu-cpuid-ebx leaf + cpu-cpuid-ecx leaf + cpu-cpuid-edx leaf

  bounded none machined : Integer
  machined = cpu-read-cr0 + cpu-read-cr3 + flush-tlb

Section: `bounded none` On An EFFECTFUL Signature

 The first declarations in this chapter that carry an effect row. `bounded
 none` is a claim about the heap and an effect row is a claim about the world,
 and nothing had ever asked whether one function may make both.

  bounded none mmio-read : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  mmio-read (addr) = read-mmio addr 0

  bounded none mmio-read-32 : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  mmio-read-32 (addr) = read-mmio-32 addr 0

  bounded none mmio-poke : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  mmio-poke (addr) (v) = poke-mmio addr 0 v

  bounded none mmio-poke-32 : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  mmio-poke-32 (addr) (v) = poke-mmio-32 addr 0 v

  bounded none ported-in : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ported-in (p) = port-in-byte p

  bounded none ported-out : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ported-out (p) (v) = port-out-byte p v

  bounded none ported-in-16 : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ported-in-16 (p) = port-in-16 p

  bounded none ported-out-16 : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ported-out-16 (p) (v) = port-out-16 p v

  bounded none ported-in-32 : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ported-in-32 (p) = port-in-32 p

  bounded none ported-out-32 : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ported-out-32 (p) (v) = port-out-32 p v

Section: The Process And Identity Reads

 Five rows with a rung and six without, and the split is the same one
 `machined` found: `process-yield`, `get-ticks`, `process-get-pid`,
 `process-count`, `hardware-random` and `boot-factstore-status` are nullary
 constants, so no call head reaches the registry for them and no declaration
 here can fail on their account. The five below are calls and do widen.

  bounded none capped : Integer -> Integer
  capped (pid) = process-get-cap pid

  bounded none statused : Integer -> Integer
  statused (pid) = process-status pid

  bounded none who : Integer -> [Identity] Integer
  who (k) = identity-whoami k

  bounded none seeded : Integer -> [Identity] Integer
  seeded (k) = device-seed k

  bounded none who-proc : Integer, Integer -> [Identity] Integer
  who-proc (p) (k) = identity-get-proc p k

Section: A Ceiling Nothing Can Breach

 `growing` is the top of the lattice and accepts anything, including a body
 that allocates. It is here so the arm covers a satisfied ceiling as well as
 a satisfying body.

  bounded growing listed : Integer -> Integer
  listed (n) = list-length (list-push [] n)

Section: The Print Family, Widened By Measurement (2026-08-25)

 The seven print builtins sat on the refusing side as `unknown` for one
 reason: nobody had measured them. `codex/test/cost/builtin-alloc` now reads
 them at zero bytes on a list of 64 and of 256, and on four different text
 shapes -- ASCII, a tier-0 accented unit, a tier-0 Cyrillic unit and a tier-1
 unit -- because `emit-print-text-loop` branches on the code unit and an ASCII
 probe never enters the multi-byte arm.

 A body whose whole job is to log was refused `bounded none` before that,
 which is a large and ordinary class. Both shapes below are refused CDX6101
 by the seed that preceded this arm.

  bounded none logs : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  logs (t) = print-line-uni t

  bounded none logs-raw : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  logs-raw (t) = print-text t

Section: Classified From The Emitter, Not The Instrument (2026-08-25)

 `codex/test/cost/builtin-alloc` cannot reach these eighteen. `vmxon` and its
 family would fault the moment an arm executed one, and the UEFI console calls
 need a firmware boot that nothing in `codex/test` performs. They are
 therefore classified the way the six proof terms already are: by reading what
 the emitter emits.

 Each is a named runtime helper in `X86_64Helpers.codex` (VMX at the `vmxon`
 record, UEFI console below it). Across the whole span that holds all
 eighteen there is no `emit-bivy-alloc`, no call to `__alloc`, and no
 `emit-call-to` at all, so none of them can allocate directly or through a
 callee. `uefi-read-key-ex` reserves a stack frame with `sub rsp, 0x58`, which
 is the stack and not the heap, and this rung is about the heap.

 These declarations are the guard, and they need no `opening` to call them:
 this chapter is compiled and not output-compared, and the cost check walks
 every definition. Each one is refused CDX6101 by the seed that preceded them.

 SEVEN MORE WERE RECLASSIFIED AND ARE DELIBERATELY NOT ARMED HERE. The eleven
 above are refused by the preceding seed; declarations over the seven NULLARY
 members of the same families -- `vmlaunch`, `vmresume`, `vmlaunch-full`,
 `vmresume-full`, `uefi-clear-screen`, `uefi-read-key`, `uefi-read-key-ex`
 -- are NOT, and the split is exactly nullary against argument-taking. The cost
 check consults a builtin's class only in CALL position, so a nullary builtin
 used as a value never has its class read at all. An arm over one of those
 would pass under both compilers and read as a check while testing nothing,
 which is the trap this file exists to avoid. Whether any nullary builtin
 actually allocates is NOT established here: the one candidate tried,
 `current-dir`, does not resolve on this target.
  bounded none vmx-on : Integer -> Integer
  vmx-on (p) = vmxon p

  bounded none vmx-clear : Integer -> Integer
  vmx-clear (p) = vmclear p

  bounded none vmx-ptrld : Integer -> Integer
  vmx-ptrld (p) = vmptrld p

  bounded none vmx-read : Integer -> Integer
  vmx-read (f) = vmread f

  bounded none vmx-write : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vmx-write (f) (v) = vmwrite f v

  bounded none msr-read : Integer -> Integer
  msr-read (m) = rdmsr m

  bounded none msr-write : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  msr-write (m) (v) = wrmsr m v

  bounded none con-attr : Integer -> Integer
  con-attr (a) = uefi-set-attr a

  bounded none con-cursor : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  con-cursor (c) (r) = uefi-set-cursor c r

  bounded none con-cursor-on : Integer -> Integer
  con-cursor-on (e) = uefi-enable-cursor e

  bounded none con-mode : Integer -> Integer
  con-mode (m) = uefi-set-mode m

Section: The Process And Channel Family (2026-08-25)

 Twenty-one more read from the emitter. All twenty-two members live in
 `X86_64IPCHelpers.codex` and `X86_64ProcessHelpers.codex`, and process memory
 does not come from the heap at all: `__spawn_pool_carve` is shift-and-add
 into a statically reserved pool, and the three out-of-family targets
 (`__idle_dispatch`, `__process_resume`, `__spawn_pool_carve`) are defined in
 `ProcessHelpers` and allocate nothing themselves. `process-get-scope` returns
 a Text but does not build one: it loads a pointer already stored in the
 process table.

 `chan-text-recv` IS THE TWENTY-SECOND AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. It
 advances the bump allocator inline -- `add r10, rax` after rounding the
 received length up -- without ever calling `__alloc` or `emit-bivy-alloc`, so
 a scan for those two names cannot see it. It stays `unknown`, the refusing
 side, because what it allocates is proportional to the MESSAGE and the
 message size is not visible in any argument, so no class here describes it.

 A spawn still consumes a fixed-pool SLOT. `none` means no HEAP, not no
 resource, and nothing in this lattice describes slot exhaustion.

  bounded none proc-wait : Integer -> Integer
  proc-wait (p) = process-wait p

  bounded none proc-scope : Integer -> Text
  proc-scope (p) = process-get-scope p

  bounded none proc-net-scope : Integer -> Text
  proc-net-scope (p) = process-get-network-scope p

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("adds 8 = " & show (adds 8))
    print-line-uni ("listed 3 = " & show (listed 3))
    print-line-uni ("seated = " & show (seated [1, 2, 3]))
    print-line-uni ("joins = " & (if joins-literals then "t" else "f") & (if joins-params True True then "t" else "f") & (if joins-comparisons 5 then "t" else "f"))
    print-line-uni ("flipped 7 = " & show (flipped 7) & " rounded 7 = " & show (rounded 7))
    print-line-uni ("reals = " & show (narrowed 7) & show (widened 7) & show (trapped 7) & show (saturated 7))
    print-line-uni ("approx = " & show (approx-trapped 7) & show (approx-saturated 7) & show (punned 7) & show (approx-punned 7))
    print-line-uni ("cpu = " & show (let c = alloc-bytes 64 in poked-16 c 258 + peeked-16 c + peeked-qword c) & " " & show (identified 0 - identified 0) & " " & show (machined - machined))
    print-line-uni ("atomics = " & show (let c = alloc-bytes 8 in poked c 5 + peeked c + bumped c 2 + swapped c 1 + (if swapped-if c 1 9 then 1 else 0) + fenced 0))
    print-line-uni ("simd = " & show (lane (vec-splat 7.0)) & " " & show (lane4 (vec4-splat (to-real-approx 7.0))) & " " & show (summed (vec-splat 3.0)) & " " & show (widthed 0.0) & " " & show (stored (alloc-bytes 16) (vec-splat 5.0)))
    logs "logs is bounded none"
  end
